---
# Both tabs mounting once each and settling is rest, not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# The sidebar's one-time auto-open of the background group: on mount,
# a tab that finds background work already running or failed opens the
# group for the person, but only the first time ever, across every tab
# of the origin — a shared localStorage flag ("bough:bg-auto") is how a
# tab remembers that some tab, at some point, already did this.
#
# Code: go/internal/serve/web/src/app.tsx lines ~433-450 (the Sidebar
# mount effect: bgRunning/bgFailed, the "bough:bg-auto" read-then-set,
# setUnfolded adding "background").
#
# Two tabs of the same origin (A, B), both open with background work
# present from their first render, each mounting its own effect once.
# The flag is shared (real localStorage); the "unfolded" fold set each
# tab shows is also localStorage-backed but each tab's React copy of it
# is read once, at that tab's own mount (readSet in useState), and nothing
# in this effect re-reads it — so a tab's own local view of "is background
# open" only ever changes by its own actions, never by the other tab's.
#
# Read and write happen in the same synchronous effect, but across two
# tabs (two separate scripts, no shared lock) the pair is not atomic
# with respect to each other: both can read the flag before either sets
# it. Split into ReadFlag then MaybeOpen to let the model checker
# explore both orderings.
#
# Where this exposes a design gap in today's code (not modelled as a
# violation — MBT against the real page, a later flow, is what would
# turn this into a failing assertion): the flag is consumed by whichever
# tab's effect runs first. The other tab's effect still finds the flag
# already set and returns, so ITS OWN sidebar never opens automatically
# even though background work was there from its own first render too.
# "Both open" (both read the flag unset before either writes) is
# harmless duplication; "neither opens" is not reachable from a single
# flag read-then-write race by itself — it takes a second, independent
# fault (a storage exception aborting the write, or the effect's
# dependency array never re-firing) to lose the auto-open entirely, and
# those are not modelled here.

role Tab:
    action Init:
        self.read = False
        self.wrote = False
        self.opened = False

    # The effect's read of the shared flag: a snapshot of what this tab
    # saw, taken before it decides whether to write.
    atomic fair action ReadFlag:
        require not self.read
        self.read = True
        self.saw = flag

    # try { if (flag === "1") return; localStorage.setItem(...) }: only a
    # tab that saw the flag unset writes it and opens locally.
    atomic fair action MaybeOpen:
        require self.read and not self.wrote
        self.wrote = True
        if self.saw == 0:
            flag = 1
            self.opened = True

action Init:
    flag = 0
    tabA = Tab()
    tabB = Tab()

# A tab only ever opens through its own MaybeOpen, and only once.
always assertion OpenedOnlyAfterWrite:
    return (not tabA.opened or tabA.wrote) and (not tabB.opened or tabB.wrote)

# The flag is set exactly when some tab's write set it; it is never
# cleared, and never set by a tab that saw it already set.
always assertion FlagFollowsAWrite:
    return flag == 0 or tabA.opened or tabB.opened

# Nobody opens twice: opening is a one-shot per tab (MaybeOpen requires
# not self.wrote, so this holds by construction, but the assertion is
# the contract, not the mechanism).
always assertion OpensAtMostOnce:
    return tabA.wrote != False or not tabA.opened

# Once both tabs have run their mount effect, the flag reflects that at
# least one of them tried, if either saw work to show: this spec always
# has work present, so both tabs having read implies the flag question
# was settled one way (a tab opened) or the other (a race left the flag
# already set from outside this pair, which two fresh tabs never do).
always eventually assertion SettledOnceBothMount:
    if tabA.read and tabB.read:
        return tabA.wrote and tabB.wrote
    return True
